{"type":"WebSearch","query":"San Francisco Chronicle endorsement Proposition 2 2026 rainy day fund Orange County Register no editorial","content":"The San Francisco Chronicle endorsed **Yes** on California Proposition 2 (2026), the measure to increase the state’s rainy day fund (Budget Stabilization Account). Their October 15, 2026, editorial, titled “Endorsement: Prop 2 could help California be more fiscally responsible — if our politicians bother to use it,” argues that doubling the reserve cap from 10% to 20% of General Fund revenues, requiring larger deposits during high capital-gains years, extending extra debt payments through 2040, and adjusting Gann Limit accounting could improve budget stability (covering roughly 50% of future shortfalls versus 30% currently). They note it is not as ambitious as Legislative Analyst’s Office recommendations but still worthwhile if lawmakers actually follow through, and they urge voters to pass it while holding politicians accountable.[[1]](https://www.sfchronicle.com/opinion/editorials/article/prop-2-money-california-22468892.php)

The Orange County Register **did publish an editorial** (contrary to any impression of none existing). Their October 7, 2026, piece, “Endorsement: No on Prop. 2. California needs fiscal discipline, not accounting tricks,” recommends voting **No**. They describe the measure as enabling more spending, expanding allowable uses of set-aside funds (including certain borrowings), extending debt-paydown rules, and creating Gann Limit loopholes that make taxpayer rebates less likely, rather than genuine fiscal restraint. They also included it in their broader 2026 proposition endorsements roundup.[[2]](https://www.ocregister.com/2026/10/07/endorsement-no-on-prop-2-california-needs-fiscal-discipline-not-accounting-tricks/)

Proposition 2 itself is a legislatively referred constitutional amendment (ACA 20) that would raise the rainy-day fund cap, capture more boom-year revenue, broaden and extend extra debt payments, and change how deposits count against the Gann spending limit. It appears on the November 3, 2026, statewide ballot.","citations":["https://www.sfchronicle.com/opinion/editorials/article/prop-2-money-california-22468892.php","https://www.ocregister.com/2026/10/07/endorsement-no-on-prop-2-california-needs-fiscal-discipline-not-accounting-tricks/"],"allowed_domains":null}
